Day 62 - Flatten a Multilevel Doubly Linked List

Problem Statement:
Given a multilevel doubly linked list where each node has next, prev, and an optional child pointer, flatten the entire structure into a single-level doubly linked list.

For a node with a child list, the child list should be inserted between the current node and its original next node.

All child pointers must be set to NULL.

Code

class Solution {
public:
    Node* flatten(Node* head) {
        if (head == NULL)
            return head;


        Node* curr = head;


        while (curr != NULL) {
            if (curr->child != NULL) {


                Node* next = curr->next;


                curr->next = flatten(curr->child);
                curr->next->prev = curr;


                curr->child = NULL;


                Node* temp = curr->next;


                while (temp->next != NULL) {
                    temp = temp->next;
                }


                if (next != NULL) {
                    temp->next = next;
                    next->prev = temp;
                }
            }


            curr = curr->next;
        }


        return head;
    }
};





Time Complexity:
O(n²) in the worst case.

Space Complexity:
O(h) due to the recursion stack, where h is the maximum depth of nested child lists.

Approach:
We traverse the list using curr. Whenever curr has a child list:

Save curr->next in next.
Recursively flatten the child list.
Connect the flattened child list after curr.
Set curr->child = NULL.
Move to the end of the flattened child list.
Connect the previously saved next node after it.
Update the prev pointers accordingly.

